<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marquesas Islands</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Marquesas Islands are a group of rugged volcanic islands in the north-east of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>, among the earliest settled in eastern Polynesia and a likely source of settlers for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Rapa Nui</a>. Visited by the Spanish navigator Álvaro de Mendaña in 1595, they were annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1842. Disease drastically reduced their population in the 19th century; the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-gauguin">Paul Gauguin</a> died on Hiva Oa in 1903.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marquesas-islands.jpg" alt="Marquesas Islands" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Background: Association MOTU HAKA Tiki's head: Association PATUTIKI Vectorization: Sémhur · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Marquesas_Islands.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Marquesas Islands, known in Marquesan as Te Henua ʻEnana or Te Fenua ʻEnata, &quot;the land of men&quot;, are among the most remote islands in the world. They lie about 1,370 kilometres north-east of Tahiti and about 4,800 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Most are volcanic, created by the Marquesas hotspot, and the highest point is Mount Oave on Ua Pou, at 1,230 metres. A northern group is centred on Nuku Hiva, and a southern group on Hiva ʻOa. Rainfall is greater on the northern and eastern windward coasts, and droughts, which seem to be linked with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-nino">El Niño</a>, are frequent and sometimes last several years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first settlers were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> from West Polynesia, descendants of the Lapita culture. Early radiocarbon dates suggested arrival before 100 CE or about 600 CE, but a 2010 study with higher-precision methods found that eastern Polynesia was settled much later, in two waves. The first reached the Society Islands, and the second, between about 1190 and 1290 CE, spread to the Marquesas. Such rapid settlement is thought to explain the close uniformity of East Polynesian culture, biology and language.</p><p>The abundant natural resources long supported a large population, which lived by fishing, collecting shellfish, hunting birds and gardening. Breadfruit was the staple, and at least 32 other introduced crops were grown. Basalt from the quarry island of Eiao was carried by sailing canoe for more than 2,500 kilometres, to supply adze heads to Moʻorea, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangareva">Mangareva</a>, Tubuai, Rarotonga and Tabuaeran.</p><p>The first Europeans to arrive may have been the crew of the Spanish ship San Lesmes, which vanished in a storm in June 1526. Álvaro de Mendaña reached the islands on 21 July 1595 and named them after his patron García Hurtado de Mendoza, Marquis of Cañete, Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. He visited Fatu Hiva and Tahuata before sailing to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon-islands">Solomon Islands</a>. The first missionaries, William Pascoe Crook and John Harris of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a>, arrived in 1797; Harris left after a few months, and Crook stayed until 1799.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Marquesas form one of the five administrative divisions of French Polynesia, with its capital at Taioha'e on Nuku Hiva. French and Tahitian are the official languages, and the Marquesan languages remain widely spoken. The islands had 9,346 inhabitants at the 2017 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and most are Catholic. In 2024 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> listed the islands as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> under the name Te Henua Enata.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marquesas Islands” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1842 France annexed the <b>Marquesas</b> and imposed a protectorate on the Kingdom of Tahiti, leading to the Franco-Tahitian War of 1844 to 1847.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-establishments-in-oceania">French Establishments in Oceania</a></li></ul></section>
